  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions & Synoptic Overview:\n\nThe cold front is approaching the Missouri/Iowa and\nKansas/Nebraska state borders early this morning, with \nscattered convection ongoing ahead of the synoptic front. \nMultiple outflow boundaries are visible on early morning radar \nimagery well south of the showers and storms, with some notable\nlightning within the stronger cores. A secondary cold front has\nbeen analyzed by WPC along a kink in the surface isobars\nextending from the surface low near James Bay in Ontario\nsouthwest across Lake Superior into central South Dakota, with \na plume of noticeably drier air visible on mid-level water \nvapor satellite imagery north of this front. This dry air is\nriding along the mid-level jet streak formed by the confluence of\nflow along the northern edge of ridging over the western US and\nthe base of an upper-level trough pivoting across northern\nOntario.\n\n\nShowers/Storms This Morning:\n\nThe outflow should act as a stabilizing force on the\nenvironment, especially west of Highway 65, and should slowly\nerode the convection moving in from southwest Iowa as well as\nexisting convection already across the area that is already\nstruggling to sustain within an increasingly capped environment.\nShowers and storms will break apart and become increasingly\nscattered as we approach sunrise and they drift further south,\nwith agreement from CAMs that the last showers should be\ndissipated from the area by 10AM. We have seen fairly efficient\nrainfall rates with some locations seeing 1-2\" per hour with \nthe strongest storms, but the progressive motion and overall \nweakening should prevent any flooding beyond temporary rate- \ndriven ponding. Isolated gusty winds will be possible through \n5AM with rain cores that get pushed down to the surface, but the\nwindow for severe weather has ended.\n\n\nTemperatures/Wind/Heat Indices Today:\n\nThe outflow from storms will help to reduce the otherwise balmy\ntemperatures early this morning, and lingering cloud cover will\nalso help the cause, though the high cloud cover associated with\nthe precip is expected to clear after the rain ends, bringing us\nto clear skies for this afternoon. The significantly cooler air\nwill lag behind the surface front a bit, meaning that southern\nportions of the area won't see significant cold air advection\nuntil near/after peak heating, and high temperatures south of\nI-70 could be similarly hot to yesterday. \n\nWhere the cold front will really provide relief is on the \nmoisture front (no pun intended). Not only will the front push \nthe 70+ degree dew points south and out of the area, winds will \ngust up to 20-25 mph this afternoon behind the front, which will\nincrease the efficiency of deep mixing and act to reduce dew \npoints even further, into the low to mid-60s! Some may have \nnoticed that the Heat Advisory has been cancelled early, and \nthis is the reason why. The front has trended faster than \nprevious guidance suggested, which means the drier air can \nprogress further south before we reach peak heating. Even though\ncentral Missouri will likely see one more day of mid- to upper \n90s temperatures this afternoon, the sub-70 dew points only \nproduce heat index values up to 100 degrees.\n\n\nSmoke:\n\nThe northwesterly flow aloft that moves into the area behind the\nfront will act as a conduit for smoke from wildfires far to our\nnorthwest to move into the area this afternoon into this\nevening, creating some hazy conditions despite mostly clear\nskies. The RRFS indicates that air quality should remain yellow\n(AQI 50-100) until the overnight nocturnal inversion sets in\ntonight. Between 12AM and 10AM on Wednesday, the corridor that\nis collocated with the most dense plume of smoke aloft may have\nAQIs above 100, indicating air quality that may be unhealthy for\nsensitive groups and potentially that you'd be able to smell\na faint smoky smell near the surface. The smoke plume should\nmove out to the southwest overnight and into Wednesday, with\nhaze clearing fully by Thursday.\n\n\nRainfall Later This Week:\n\nDespite the shift to northwesterly flow, there are several\nrounds of rain chances before week's end. A series of shortwaves\nwill send vort maxes riding across the northern edge of the \nanticyclonic circulation in the mid- and upper levels of the \natmosphere, which is located across Nebraska on Wednesday and\ncentral Missouri on Thursday. These energy pulses will lead to\nrepeated rounds of rainfall/mesoscale convective complexes\nWednesday-Friday, with heavy rainfall looking increasingly\nplausible in association with this activity. Rain chances sit\nat 30-60% Wednesday night into Thursday morning in areas along\nand west of I-49, and 45-80% Thursday night into the day Friday\nareawide, lingering at 15-40% in southern portions of the area\ninto Friday night.\n\nWe begin to see more moisture return aloft into our area Thursday\nand Friday, with a more favorable southwesterly wind for \nmoisture advection arriving as the embedded",
